# The site builds with npm. Its recorded fixtures under src/data/generated/
# are committed on purpose - they are evidence, not build output.
site/node_modules/
site/dist/
site/.smoke/
site/.lint/
site/.prerender/
site/.visual/
site/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# Conformance harnesses install their own dependencies.
conformance/a2a-js/node_modules/
conformance/a2a-js/package-lock.json
conformance/a2a-go/refagent
conformance/a2a-java/target/
conformance/a2a-dotnet/bin/
conformance/a2a-dotnet/obj/

# Local agent, editor and operating-system state. These were ignored on one
# machine, by that machine's global ignore file, which a clone does not
# inherit - so the first fork to run a coding agent is offered its local
# settings to commit. `.claude/` itself is deliberately not ignored: a
# checked-in `settings.json` is shareable and belongs in review. Only the
# per-machine files do not.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/*.local.json
CLAUDE-SECURITY-*/
.idea/
.vscode/
*.code-workspace
*.swp
Thumbs.db

# Coverage writes `.coverage.<host>.<pid>` when run in parallel, which the
# bare name above does not catch.
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml

# The Vercel CLI, if anyone links the site locally.
site/.vercel/

# Scratch space for moving the repo into a sandbox. Never committed: a
# 29 MB tarball of the repo reached history once through `git add -A`.
_to_delete/

# Archives, wherever they land. The 29 MB blob that reached history was a
# tarball in the repo root swept up by `git add -A`; ignoring the folder it
# happened to sit in only covers the name it happened to use.
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.zip

# Review correspondence. Verification passes, retests and findings reports are
# working notes between a maintainer and a reviewer: useful while the work is
# happening, stale within a release, and not documentation anyone cloning this
# needs. What they established lives in CHANGELOG.md, in the commit messages,
# and in the guards those passes produced.
#
# Anchored prefixes are why this needed a third pass: `validation-*.md` matched
# the file it was written for and not `pypi-validation-0.1.2.md`, which sat
# untracked and unignored where any `git add -A` would have taken it. These are
# unanchored for that reason — the report is the same kind of file whatever it
# is called, and the pattern should not depend on remembering the convention.
#
# `*-review.md` is here for a sharper reason than staleness. The code review it
# was added for reproduces a credential leak, so it quotes a key-shaped literal
# four times. The key is fabricated, but a public repository is a poor place to
# find that out, and history is expensive to rewrite. A reviewer's reproduction
# of a secrets bug is exactly the file that must never be committed by accident.
#
# The list grew twice more, and the second time it was already too late: two
# reports had been committed by a `git add -A` that nobody read the output of,
# each riding into a commit about something unrelated. Patterns cannot keep up
# with what a reviewer decides to call a file, which is why the real guard is
# `test_no_review_correspondence_is_committed` in `tests/test_packaging.py` —
# an allowlist of the markdown this repository actually ships, checked against
# what is tracked.
*FINDINGS*.md
*retest-*.md
*validation-*.md
*-audit.md
*-review.md
*-result.md
*-to-test-against.md
